---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import ProjectGrid from '../../components/ProjectGrid.astro';
import Shot from '../../components/Shot.astro';
import LinkOut from '../../components/LinkOut.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';

export function getStaticPaths() {
	return projects.map((project) => ({
		params: { slug: project.slug },
		props: { project },
	}));
}

const { project } = Astro.props;

/* `images` is one shape now — { src, w, h, caption? } — so there is nothing to
   flatten here any more (2026-08-17; the bare-string form and the map that
   normalised it went together). See the field's note in projects.ts for why a
   caption and a size both ride on their own src rather than in parallel
   arrays, and why the string form had to go. */
/* Sectioned studies (2026-10-07) keep their shots inside their sections; the
   flat run is still needed so the lightbox can count "image N of M" across
   the whole page. Same objects, so a section can find its own index in it. */
const figures = project.sections ? project.sections.flatMap((s) => s.images) : project.images;

/* The link-out's text: the written label where there is one, else the URL's
   host with a leading "www." dropped (2026-09-30, off the feedback that a bare
   link didn't render at all). Host, not the full URL — stacksmith's link is a
   Subframe share path ~90 characters long and .btn-quiet cannot wrap (see the
   field's note in projects.ts for the ~27-character ceiling); the host is the
   part a reader decides on anyway. `new URL` throws on a malformed link, which
   is the right failure — at build time, on the entry that is wrong. */
const linkLabel =
	project.linkLabel ??
	(project.link ? new URL(project.link).hostname.replace(/^www\./, '') : undefined);

/* The caption's type is the prose's, exactly (2026-08-14, Hiro's ask): the same
   text-sm / leading-relaxed the two paragraphs above carry, with no colour and
   no weight of its own, so it inherits --ink and --weight-body the same way they
   do. That is the whole rule — if the prose moves, these follow, and this const
   should never grow a size or colour the paragraphs don't have.

   ⚠ It briefly ran at 0.7rem on --ink-soft, the about page's figcaption. That
   is the obvious treatment and it was overruled deliberately, so don't restore
   it as a fix: these are not descriptive captions, they are the labels the
   before/after argument turns on, and a label set quieter than the prose it is
   evidence for reads as an aside. The one thing worth keeping from that pass is
   what it was fixing — UNSTYLED, a figcaption inherits the 19px root and comes
   out BIGGER than the 16.625px prose. text-sm is what stops that; empty is not.

   Margin is BOTTOM because the caption sits above its image (see the template).
   0.625rem is 11.875px against the section's space-y-5 of 23.75px, so a label
   is always twice as close to the image it names as to the figure before it.
   That 2:1 is doing all the grouping work now that the type carries no contrast
   of its own — keep the ratio if the outer gap ever moves. */
/* The class itself moved to Shot.astro with the markup (2026-10-07); the
   argument above is still the whole reason it is what it is. */

/* ── THREE CARDS, THE THREE THAT FOLLOW THIS ONE (2026-08-16, Hiro: the strip
   should only show three projects) ──
   Not the first three of the list with the current one removed, and that
   distinction is the entire reason this is six lines instead of a .slice(0, 3)
   on the filter. Taking the top of a fixed list means the projects at the
   BOTTOM of projects.ts are never in it: at five projects, plain
   filter().slice(0, 3) shows migaki/ejs/stacksmith almost everywhere and
   renders Shipyard on exactly zero pages — the last entry can only be reached
   from the home grid, and the strip is the page's only way onward. A rule that
   makes one project unreachable is not a shortening of the list, it is a
   quiet unpublishing of the work at the end of it.

   Wrapping fixes that arithmetically rather than by taste: every project is
   the successor of exactly three others, so every project appears in exactly
   three strips, and clicking the FIRST card each time walks you through all
   five in projects.ts order and returns you to where you started. That is a
   real reading path, which is more than a "related work" list of any three.

   THE ORDER IS STILL projects.ts, ROTATED — never re-sorted. Same order the
   home grid uses, so the strip reads as that grid resumed from where you are
   rather than as a second, differently-sorted list. NOT sorted by year or
   "relatedness": the tags field isn't rendered anywhere (off since 07-29,
   back for a few hours on 09-30, see the header note), and any similarity
   metric over a handful of items is theatre. The one visible
   cost is the wrap point — on the last project the strip runs migaki, ejs,
   stacksmith, so the row reads out of list order once per cycle. That is the
   price of every project being reachable and it is worth it.

   ⚠ NOT RANDOM, and don't make it so to "keep it fresh": this is a static
   build, so a random pick is frozen at build time anyway — it would just be an
   arbitrary fixed answer with no reason attached, and it would silently
   reintroduce the unreachable-project bug on some builds and not others.

   The strip asks ProjectGrid for three columns (2026-08-11, Hiro). That
   coupling is HAND-MAINTAINED, not derived: `columns` is a literal 3 down in
   the strip, not others.length. Deriving it was the obvious move and is the
   wrong one — the grid stops at three columns because a fourth shrinks the
   cards past their titles (ProjectGrid.astro has the measurements). The two
   threes now agree by construction, which is new: from 08-11 to today they did
   not, and four cards rendered three across with ONE ALONE ON ROW TWO. That
   ragged second row is what this change removes, and the note it replaces had
   chosen it deliberately over exactly this fix, on the grounds that capping
   the strip trades a ragged row for hiding work. The wrap is what settles that
   argument — nothing is hidden if everything is three clicks from everything.

   BELOW FIVE PROJECTS this simply yields fewer than three: the slice takes
   what there is, and at four projects it is still a full row. At two the
   strip disappears entirely — see the guard on the section below. */
const current = projects.findIndex((other) => other.slug === project.slug);
const others = [
	...projects.slice(current + 1),
	...projects.slice(0, current),
].slice(0, 3);
---

<Layout title={`${project.title} — Hiro Eernisse`} description={project.blurb}>
	<Nav />

	{/* ── sm:pb-16 (2026-08-11, Hiro) ──
	    All three routes end on the same footer element, so they should end on
	    the same amount of air, and this one was a step tighter than the other
	    two: the footer's own py-10 (47.5px) sits on top of whatever the page
	    leaves below its last block, and that was py-12's 57px here against 76px
	    on both home (#projects pb-16) and about (main sm:py-16) — 104.5px to
	    their 123.5px.

	    BOTTOM ONLY, deliberately asymmetric, which is why this is sm:pb-16 and
	    not about's symmetric sm:py-16: the top padding sets where the h1 lands
	    on arrival, and that is a settled decision this has no business moving.
	    The complaint was the footer.

	    ⚠ BELOW sm the three still disagree, and that is pre-existing rather than
	    something this introduced: home's pb-16 is unconditional (123.5px at
	    every width) while about's step is sm:py-16, so about and this page both
	    sit at 104.5px on a phone. Matching there means changing the home page or
	    about, not this file — left alone because the ask was this page's gap. */}
	<main class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-12 sm:pb-16">
		{/* No back link up here on purpose. It used to be that the sticky nav's
		    Projects link never left the viewport, so a second one this close read
		    as two of the same control; that link is gone (08-01), and the reason
		    is now simply that the footer's does the job better — it returns you
		    to the card you came from, at the scroll position you left it. */}

		{/* ── 2026-09-30: THE DATELINE CARRIES COMPANY AND STATUS ──
		    year · company · status, one --ink-soft line above the title. Off a
		    review that asked for company, role, status and tags on every page;
		    company had been off the header since 07-29 on the argument that it
		    reads off the prose — it does, eventually, and a reader arriving from
		    a card wants it before the prose. Status is the one word a reviewer
		    is actually asking for: did this ship.

		    ⚠ ROLE AND TAGS WERE RENDERED HERE FOR A FEW HOURS AND CAME OFF
		    (Hiro, same day: "super busy", "cognitive overload"). The first cut
		    was four lines — dateline, title, the role sentence in prose type, a
		    status · tags line — and the block competed with the title instead
		    of serving it. What made it heavy was not the line count alone but
		    duplication: every project's role sentence is the first sentence of
		    its work paragraph in shorter form, so a reader paid a line for a
		    fact they get one screen later. And the tags sort nothing on a
		    five-project site — "Product Design · UI/UX" is filler, and the one
		    tag that carried information ("Open Source") had become the status.
		    Both fields stay in projects.ts, unrendered, as they were before.
		    If they are ever wanted back, the bar is that they say something the
		    prose does not.

		    No labels in front of any of it — "Status:" — the site dropped its
		    kickers on 07-29 and a key-value list is the same shape lying down.
		    Position does the labelling: a word beside a year and a company is
		    the state of that engagement. The middots are text, not markup: three
		    words are one phrase, not a list a screen reader needs announced.

		    COMPANY IS OPTIONAL (2026-10-03, Hiro: "Independent · Open source is
		    redundant"). A self-initiated project has no one it was for, and the
		    word that filled the slot said what the status already says. The
		    middot goes with it, so the line is two words instead of three — not
		    an empty slot between two dots. Year and status always render.

		    ── 2026-08-10: THIS PAGE TAKES THE ABOUT PAGE'S SCALE AND PALETTE ──
		    Hiro's ask, and it applies to the whole page, so read this note before
		    changing any size or colour below. The project page had been running
		    its own type scale (title 35.625/42.75px, lead 23.75, body 21.375) and
		    its own two accents (--ink-title on the h1, --ink-num on the year).
		    The about page runs THREE values and no accents: headings at
		    --ink-heading, prose at 16.625px in plain --ink, ancillary text at
		    --ink-soft. That set is now this page's set too.

		    --ink-soft on the year, not --ink-num. The about page has no numerals,
		    so there was no counterpart to match — what it does have is small
		    ancillary text (the photo caption, the footer) and that runs
		    --ink-soft. The year rode ON the muted line already and the orange was
		    there to lift it out of it, which is the thing that goes.
		    ⚠ This was --ink-num's LAST use on the site; the card datelines dropped
		    it on 08-02 (see ProjectCard.astro). The token is still defined in
		    global.css and nothing consumes it. Left there rather than deleted —
		    that is a palette decision, not a page one.

		    NO sm:text-4xl. The h1 sat one step larger from sm up; it is one size
		    at every width now, 28.5px on a phone and on a desktop alike.

		    ── 2026-08-11: text-2xl, NOT the about page's text-3xl ──
		    Hiro's ask, "make the heading smaller", one step down the scale
		    (35.625 → 28.5px). This is the first place the two pages part company
		    since yesterday's pass above, and the note stays because that pass is
		    what a later reader will find first: the SCALE ANCHOR is no longer
		    "whatever the about page's greeting is". Colour, weight and tracking
		    are still that page's exactly; only this one number is ours.

		    It still clears the strip's h2 (text-xl / 23.75px) by a step, which is
		    what the page needs it to do — the h1 is the only text above the fold
		    and the h2 is several thousand pixels below it, so the pair never
		    shares a screen, but the two are the page's only headings and reading
		    them as one scale rather than two is the point. text-xl here would
		    have flattened that and is the step to avoid if "smaller" comes up
		    again; the honest next move at that point is dropping the h2 too. */}
		<header class="reveal" style="--i: 0">
			<p class="text-sm text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
				<span class="tabular-nums">{project.year}</span>{project.company && ` · ${project.company}`} · {project.status}
			</p>
			<h1 class="mt-2 text-2xl [font-weight:var(--weight-emph)] tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">{project.title}</h1>
		</header>

		{/* Reading matter is capped at --measure (see global.css) — ~11 words a
		    line — while the page itself spans the nav's full 960px, which the
		    images below fill.

		    No "Background" / "Work" headings: two paragraphs in one screen give
		    a reader nothing to skip to, and the labels were the same small-bold
		    kicker shape the home page dropped.

		    ⚠ THE LEAD IS GONE (2026-08-10, with the about-page scale — see the
		    header note). The register change the dropped headings used to mark
		    — context, then what I did — rode the TYPE from 08-01 on: the problem
		    paragraph was a step up in size (23.75px) and set in --ink-soft, so
		    the work paragraph read as the body it stepped down into. The about
		    page sets every paragraph identically, so matching it spends that
		    device: both are now 16.625px in plain --ink and nothing but the
		    paragraph break separates them. That is a real loss and it was the
		    price of the ask, not an oversight — if the two paragraphs ever need
		    telling apart again, the headings are the honest fix, because the
		    type has no step left to make.

		    Nothing here is capped by these numbers, but the MEASURE now is: the
		    section is still max-w-[var(--measure)] (35rem / 665px), which held
		    ~11 words a line at 21.375px and holds ~80 characters at 16.625 —
		    past the usual 45–75. The about page's own column is 511.5px and ~62
		    characters. Narrowing this is the obvious follow-up and was left
		    alone deliberately: it is a layout change, and the ask was sizes and
		    colours. The page itself still spans the nav's full 960px, which the
		    images below fill. */}
		{/* ── Two shapes of page (2026-10-07, off the feedback to take two studies
		    to full depth and leave the rest short) ──
		    A project with `sections` renders them: heading, one paragraph, its
		    shots, repeated; the link out closes the reading the same way it does
		    below. Everything else takes the short form that follows, untouched.
		    The two forms share Shot and LinkOut so a change to either control is
		    one change.

		    Rhythm inside a study: h2, mt-3 to the paragraph (the pair is one
		    unit), mt-6 to the shots (28.5px, tighter than the short form's
		    47.5px prose-to-screenshots gap because here the picture belongs to
		    THIS paragraph and nothing else), mt-14 (66.5px) to the next section
		    so a section's own gaps are always smaller than the gap to its
		    neighbour. The paragraph keeps --measure; the shots take the full
		    920px like every other figure on the site. The whole study is ONE
		    reveal beat (--i: 1): eight sections on the cascade would run past a
		    second, and everything after the first shot is below the fold anyway.

		    h2 is the "More projects" h2 one step down (text-lg against text-xl):
		    same weight, same ink, same tracking, so the page has two heading
		    sizes under the h1 and they rank by size alone. */}
		{project.sections ? (
			<div class="reveal mt-10" style="--i: 1">
				{project.sections.map(({ heading, body, images }) => (
					<section class="mt-14 first:mt-0">
						{/* No heading on the opening section (2026-10-07, Hiro): an h2
						    straight under the h1 read as a subtitle. The paragraph then
						    drops its mt-3 so it sits at the container's mt-10 from the
						    title, the same gap the short form opens with. */}
						{heading && (
							<h2 class="text-lg [font-weight:var(--weight-emph)] tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">
								{heading}
							</h2>
						)}
						<p class:list={['max-w-[var(--measure)] text-sm leading-relaxed', heading && 'mt-3']}>{body}</p>
						{images.length > 0 && (
							<div class="mt-6 space-y-5">
								{images.map((image) => (
									<Shot
										{...image}
										index={figures.indexOf(image) + 1}
										total={figures.length}
										title={project.title}
									/>
								))}
							</div>
						)}
					</section>
				))}
				{project.link && (
					<div class="mt-14">
						<LinkOut href={project.link} label={linkLabel} />
					</div>
				)}
			</div>
		) : (
			<>
		<section class="reveal mt-8 max-w-[var(--measure)]" style="--i: 1">
			{/* ── 2026-08-11: no weight override, so these inherit --weight-body ──
			    Hiro's ask, "make the body font weight lighter". Both paragraphs
			    carried [font-weight:var(--weight-ui)] (w400 dark / w500 light);
			    dropping it takes them to w300/w400. This is the change the
			    previous note here named as the exact-match move — the paragraphs
			    now render at the about page's size, colour AND weight, and this
			    page has no type of its own left except the h1's size.

			    ⚠ WHAT THAT OVERRIDE WAS HOLDING, because it was not decoration:
			    it was the 2026-08-04 legibility audit's finding, the only weight
			    bump that audit required, and the one thing the 08-10 about-page
			    pass deliberately left standing on the argument that weight is
			    neither a size nor a colour and this is the site's longest-form
			    reading. That argument has now been overruled by the ask, which is
			    Hiro's call to make; it is recorded here so the bump is not
			    re-applied as a "fix" by someone who finds the audit and not this.

			    The audit's own numbers are STALE and are not restated: they were
			    measured at 23.75px/--ink-soft and 21.375px/--ink, and both
			    paragraphs are 16.625px/--ink now. What survives the restatement is
			    the direction — smaller type needs MORE weight for the same APCA
			    floor, and this went the other way at 16.625px, so assume the
			    margin is thinner than anything the audit recorded. Re-measure
			    before quoting a figure.

			    It is not an unprecedented posture: the about page's prose is
			    16.625px at w300 in this same ink, and the audit already carries
			    that as a standing contrast item weight cannot fix. This page has
			    joined it rather than invented a worse one. If the floor ever has
			    to be met for real, --ink-heading on the prose buys more than any
			    weight tier does at this size, and colour is the lever the 08-10
			    pass already used to retire the lead's shortfall. */}
			<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">{project.problem}</p>
			<p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed">{project.work}</p>

			{/* ── The link out (2026-08-16, Hiro: add links to migaki and ejs.co on
			    the respective project pages; 2026-09-30: on every page with a URL) ──
			    It renders off `link`. Until 09-30 it rendered off `linkLabel`,
			    which kept three of the five URLs unpublished by design — the
			    08-16 ask named two pages — and the 09-30 feedback is that a bare
			    link should still render. So the label is now derived where none
			    is written (see `linkLabel` in the frontmatter, and the field's
			    note in projects.ts for what to write when the host is the wrong
			    word). To publish no link, omit the URL; there is no other switch.

			    ── WHY .btn-quiet, and why this is not the Visit button returning ──
			    That style has been ORPHANED since 08-10, when the back-to-projects
			    link (its only consumer) came off this page, and its note in
			    global.css left it standing on the explicit grounds that "the next
			    borderless text link on the site wants exactly this". This is that
			    link. It arrives with the whole family already decided — 0.875rem,
			    --weight-ui, the site's one underline (instant in, --dur-pop-out
			    out), a focus ring — so there is no new treatment on the page.

			    ── IT IS CYAN (2026-08-16, Hiro), which is the one thing the
			    family does NOT decide for it ──
			    Hence the --out modifier: --ink-link at rest instead of the
			    family's --ink-soft, and no colour change on hover, because an
			    accent has nowhere to darken TO — the family's hover ink is a grey.
			    Read .btn-quiet--out in global.css for the argument, and for why
			    its hover rule looks like a no-op and isn't. What the colour buys
			    on THIS page is worth stating: the link sits a few lines above a
			    figcaption set at the same size, and --ink-soft separated the two
			    by half a step of grey. Cyan separates them by kind, which is what
			    they actually differ by.

			    The 07-29 removal that took the tags also took a "Visit" button,
			    and the thing that made it worth removing was that it was a BOXED
			    .btn: the only piece of enclosed chrome on an otherwise borderless
			    layout. That is the reason .btn-quiet exists at all. Borderless is
			    the fix, so this is not a reversal of that call.

			    THE ARROW IS THE ↗, and the family's note names this exact glyph as
			    the one meaning it carries: this leaves the site. It is also the
			    site's existing signal for target="_blank" (Nav.astro says so at the
			    contact slider), so the mark and the behaviour agree. No tilt on it
			    — a rotation would encode a second meaning and there isn't one; the
			    only arrow on the site that turns is the projects toggle, where the
			    turn IS open-vs-shut. And no hover nudge: nothing on this control
			    moves for a cursor (2026-08-16, and the family lost its nudge the
			    same day).

			    Drawn in the family's idiom — 24 box, line + polyline, stroke 2,
			    round caps — spanning 5→19 rather than a stock icon's 7→17. That is
			    optical, not cosmetic: at width 14 the shaft has to cover roughly
			    the same fraction of its box as the toggle's ↓ (which runs 4→19) or
			    a diagonal reads a size smaller sitting next to it. The head's legs
			    are 10 units, which is the right arrow's 9.9 to the pixel.

			    ── PLACEMENT: end of the prose, not the header and not the foot ──
			    Inside this section, so it rides the prose's own --i: 1 beat and
			    enters as part of the block it belongs to rather than as a fifth
			    thing. It is also inside --measure, so it starts on the same left
			    edge as every line above it.
			    NOT in the header: that is a settled two-line composition (year,
			    title) and a link is a third register in it.
			    NOT below the screenshots: the only gap down there is the 114px
			    before "More projects", which was measured as an empty span and
			    reads as one — a control parked in it would both break that and
			    compete with the strip for the same click.

			    mt-8 (38px) is chosen against its neighbours, which is the only way
			    this page sets a gap: 28.5px between the two paragraphs, 47.5px from
			    here to the screenshots. Sitting between the two makes the link
			    further from the prose than the prose is from itself, and closer to
			    it than the images are — so it reads as the end of the reading, not
			    as a section of its own. The wrapper is a block <div> because
			    .btn-quiet is inline-flex; margin on an atomic inline works but
			    lands in a line box, and the div keeps the gap the plain 38px this
			    note claims. */}
			{project.link && (
				<div class="mt-8">
					<LinkOut href={project.link} label={linkLabel} />
				</div>
			)}
		</section>

		<section class="reveal mt-10 space-y-5" style="--i: 2">
			{/* Placeholder frames for a project whose screenshots don't exist yet.
			    Same border, radius and bed as the real images below, but at the
			    cards' 16/9 — a real screenshot sets its own height from the file,
			    and an empty div has none to take, so the ratio has to be stated.
			    They hold the page's shape (and the reveal's third beat) so
			    dropping the images in later is a data change, nothing more. */}
			{project.images.length === 0 &&
				Array.from({ length: project.placeholders ?? 0 }).map(() => (
					<div class="aspect-[16/9] w-full rounded-[var(--radius)] border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--surface-sunken)] bg-clip-padding" />
				))}
			{/* ── The screenshots open (2026-08-14, Hiro: hovering an image should
			    give the zoom cursor, clicking should open it full screen) ──
			    Each shot is wrapped in a real <button>, not a click handler on the
			    <img>. The wrapper is what makes the gesture reachable by keyboard
			    and announced as a control; an img with a listener is neither, and
			    this page's screenshots are the only thing on it worth enlarging.
			    The button is a bare block box (see .shot in global.css) — the
			    border, radius and bed still belong to the <img>, so the rendered
			    page is pixel-identical to what it was before the wrapper existed.

			    The label names the POSITION ("image 2 of 5") rather than repeating
			    the alt text underneath it, which the button already exposes; a
			    screen reader reaching this hears the alt, then what the control
			    does with it. It rides in data-shot-label rather than in an
			    aria-label because the resting state has to carry no ARIA at all —
			    see the note on the button itself for why.

			    THE BUTTON IS A BUTTON ON EVERY DEVICE (2026-10-03, Hiro: "add
			    lightbox for when tapped"). From 08-14 it was promoted only where a
			    fine pointer existed — a phone was to pinch-zoom the page instead —
			    and on 09-30 a reader reported the screenshots illegible there all
			    the same. A sideways-scrolling figure was tried for a day and taken
			    out ("it should not horizontally scroll"); the lightbox is the
			    answer on touch too. The markup still ships INERT (see the button's
			    own note) and the script promotes it once it runs; what the device
			    changes is how the open modal behaves, not whether a tap opens it.

			    ── The <figure> wrapper and the caption (merged in from the caption
			    branch, 2026-08-14) ──
			    figure > figcaption + button > img, and that NESTING IS THE WHOLE
			    RESOLUTION. The caption sits outside the button on purpose: inside,
			    it would join the button's hit area — so clicking a label would open
			    a lightbox — and it would be swallowed by the control's accessible
			    name. Outside, it stays a caption of the figure and the button stays
			    a control wrapping exactly one image. Don't collapse these two
			    wrappers into one; they answer different questions.

			    CAPTION FIRST, above the image. Spec-legal — figcaption may be the
			    figure's first or last child — and it earns the position: a reader
			    cannot un-see a picture, so a label underneath arrives after the
			    impression it was meant to frame, which for a before/after is
			    exactly backwards.

			    The figure is now the section's space-y-5 child, which is what gives
			    the caption a gap of its own to be tighter than — the one inside the
			    figure has to stay well under that 5.

			    The markup itself is Shot.astro (2026-10-07), one figure per call, and
			    the notes that ride on its attributes went with it. */}
			{
				figures.map((image, i) => (
					<Shot {...image} index={i + 1} total={figures.length} title={project.title} />
				))
			}
		</section>
			</>
		)}

