	{/* ── The lightbox (2026-08-14, Hiro) ──
	    ONE dialog for the whole page, re-pointed at whichever shot was clicked,
	    rather than one per image. There is only ever one open, the markup for a
	    fourth screenshot would be identical to the first three, and a single
	    element means a single place for the dismissal handler to live.

	    ── ON A POINTER, IT CLOSES WHEREVER YOU CLICK; ON TOUCH, THE PICTURE
	    IS EXEMPT AND A CLOSE CHIP COMES BACK (2026-08-14, revised 2026-10-03) ──
	    With a cursor the zoom cursor is the entire control surface: zoom-in over
	    a shot on the page, zoom-out over every pixel of the open modal, image
	    included. The gesture reverses itself, so the thing that opened it closes
	    it, and there is no chip — a cursor already says "this puts it back" on
	    every pixel, and a button would be a second answer to that question.

	    On touch none of that exists. The chip that shipped for a few hours on
	    08-14 was always FOR touch — a cursor cannot be an affordance where there
	    is no cursor — and it went only because touch itself was scoped out that
	    day on the argument that a phone pinch-zooms the page. That argument
	    failed a real reader (09-30), so touch is back in and the chip with it,
	    touch-only (.lightbox__close in global.css). The picture is not a dismiss
	    target there: it is the thing being panned, and a tap landing between
	    two pans must not throw the viewer out. Scrim and chip close; the image
	    does not. The pointer side keeps its accepted cost — clicking a
	    screenshot to point at something in it closes it.

	    A NATIVE <dialog> opened with showModal(), which is doing more work here
	    than it looks. Four behaviours come free and are NOT reimplemented below,
	    so read this before "adding" any of them:
	      · Esc closes it (the platform's close request);
	      · it renders in the TOP LAYER, above everything, so it needs no z-index
	        and cannot be trapped by the nav's stacking context or the page's
	        transformed .reveal blocks — either of which would defeat a plain
	        fixed-position div;
	      · the rest of the page goes inert, so a tab press can't land on a nav
	        link behind the image, and focus returns to the shot you opened when
	        it closes;
	      · ::backdrop is a real element to paint, so the scrim isn't a div.

	    closedby="any" asks the browser for LIGHT DISMISS — a click on the
	    backdrop closes it with no script at all. With the whole modal now a
	    dismiss target the script covers that case anyway, so this is belt and
	    braces rather than the mechanism; it stays because it means the scrim
	    keeps working if the module never loads. It is still why the dialog
	    shrink-wraps the image (see .lightbox in global.css) instead of filling
	    the viewport: "outside" has to be outside the dialog's own box for the
	    browser to count it.

	    FOCUS ON ARRIVAL: showModal() focuses the first focusable descendant,
	    which on touch is the chip and on a pointer device — where the chip is
	    display: none and so unfocusable — the dialog element itself. Either
	    way the aria-label below is what a screen reader announces, and Esc is
	    the way out without a pointer; on Android the back gesture is the same
	    close request. */}
	<dialog id="lightbox" class="lightbox" closedby="any" aria-label="Enlarged screenshot">
		{/* src and alt are both set by the script — empty here so the page never
		    fetches a second copy of a screenshot nobody has asked to enlarge. */}
		<img class="lightbox__img" alt="" />
		{/* Touch-only, hidden by CSS everywhere a cursor exists — see the note
		    above and .lightbox__close. AFTER the image so it paints over it
		    without a z-index; it is fixed to the viewport, so it stays put while
		    the picture pans underneath. The glyph is the site's stroke idiom
		    (2px, round caps), same as the link-out arrow above. */}
		<button type="button" class="lightbox__close" aria-label="Close">
			<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true" width="16" height="16">
				<line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"></line>
				<line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"></line>
			</svg>
		</button>
	</dialog>
</Layout>

<script>
	const dialog = document.getElementById('lightbox') as HTMLDialogElement | null;
	const full = dialog?.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>('.lightbox__img');

	if (dialog && full) {
		// ── Every device gets the lightbox (2026-10-03, Hiro) ──
		// From 08-14 to 10-03 the whole feature was gated on the query below: a
		// phone was to pinch-zoom the page instead, and the buttons stayed inert
		// there. A reader found the screenshots illegible on a phone all the
		// same, a sideways-scrolling figure was tried for a day and taken out
		// ("it should not horizontally scroll"), and the lightbox is the answer
		// on touch too. The query is still read, but it now decides HOW the open
		// modal dismisses, not whether a tap opens it.
		//
		// `(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)` is the pair, not either alone:
		// hover alone lets in a stylus, and pointer alone lets in a TV remote.
		// Both together mean a mouse or a trackpad — the device class where a
		// cursor can carry the affordance the pointer-side dismissal relies on.
		// Read live, inside the handler rather than branched on at startup, so
		// an iPad that gains a trackpad mid-session simply follows the device.
		const pointer = matchMedia('(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)');

		const shots = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-shot]');

		// The markup ships every <button> INERT — disabled, role="presentation",
		// label parked in data-shot-label — because the module is deferred and a
		// touch screen reader would otherwise announce one control per figure
		// that does nothing until it lands. Promotion is one pass, every device.
		//
		// ⚠ ALL THREE ATTRIBUTES MOVE TOGETHER (2026-08-18). ARIA's
		// presentational-roles conflict resolution ignores role="presentation"
		// on a focusable element or one carrying a global ARIA attribute, so
		// the resting state needs `disabled` AND the label off the element for
		// the role to apply at all; promotion reverses all three. tabIndex is
		// not a fourth member — `disabled` decides focusability both ways.
		shots.forEach((shot) => {
			shot.disabled = false;
			shot.removeAttribute('role');
			shot.setAttribute('aria-label', shot.dataset.shotLabel ?? '');

			shot.addEventListener('click', () => {
				const img = shot.querySelector('img');
				if (!img) return;
				// currentSrc, not src: it is the file the browser actually chose and
				// already has in cache, so the enlarged view paints instantly instead
				// of starting a second request. They are the same string today (no
				// srcset on these), which is exactly why this is worth stating — the
				// day a srcset appears, this keeps working.
				full.src = img.currentSrc || img.src;
				full.alt = img.alt;
				dialog.showModal();
				// On touch the dialog is a scroll container (see global.css) and a
				// closed dialog KEEPS its scroll offset — reopened on the next shot
				// it would come up panned to wherever the last one was left, with
				// the picture's start off-screen and the scrim band under the
				// picture instead of beside it. Start every open at the origin.
				// A no-op on a pointer device, where nothing overflows.
				dialog.scrollTo(0, 0);
			});
		});

		// ── Dismissal ──
		// On a pointer device a click ANYWHERE closes it — scrim or picture, no
		// target check — because the zoom-out cursor runs over the whole modal
		// and promises exactly that (see .lightbox in global.css). On touch the
		// picture is EXEMPT: there is no cursor to make that promise, the image
		// is the thing being panned, and a tap landing between two pans must not
		// throw the viewer out. The scrim around it and the close chip (touch
		// only, .lightbox__close) close, as does the platform's own close
		// request — Esc, and the back gesture on Android. A pan never reaches
		// here: a touch scroll cancels the click.
		//
		// ⚠ An older version tracked pointerdown and pointerup separately so a
		// drag that started on the image and ended on the scrim didn't count as
		// a scrim click. That mattered only while the image was NOT a dismiss
		// target on a pointer device, which it has been since 08-14. Don't bring
		// it back for the touch exemption — a tap is a click on ONE target.
		dialog.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
			if (!pointer.matches && e.target === full) return;
			dialog.close();
		});
	}
</script>
